    "outcome": "Infrared absorption by greenhouse gases (resonance model)",
    "stem": {
     "type": "doc",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "paragraph",
       "content": [
        {
         "marks": [],
         "type": "text",
         "text": "Carbon dioxide in the atmosphere absorbs infrared radiation emitted by the surface of the Earth."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "type": "paragraph",
       "content": [
        {
         "marks": [],
         "type": "text",
         "text": "What is the reason that carbon dioxide molecules absorb this radiation strongly?"
        }
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    "stimuli": [],
    "mcq": {
     "option_kind": "statement",
     "option_layout": "single_value",
     "column_headers": [],
     "statements": [],
     "options": [
      {
       "key": "A",
       "cells": [
        {
         "type": "doc",
         "content": [
          {
           "type": "paragraph",
           "content": [
            {
             "marks": [],
             "type": "text",
             "text": "The frequency of the radiation matches a natural frequency of vibration of the molecules."
            }
           ]
          }
         ]
        }
       ],
       "rationale": null
      },
      {
       "key": "B",
       "cells": [
        {
         "type": "doc",
         "content": [
          {
           "type": "paragraph",
           "content": [
            {
             "marks": [],
             "type": "text",
             "text": "The photons of the radiation have enough energy to ionize the molecules."
            }
           ]
          }
         ]
        }
       ],
       "rationale": {
        "type": "doc",
        "content": [
         {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
           {
            "marks": [],
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Absorption is confused with ionization; the energy of an infrared photon is far too small for ionization"
           }
          ]
         }
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "key": "C",
       "cells": [
        {
         "type": "doc",
         "content": [
          {
           "type": "paragraph",
           "content": [
            {
             "marks": [],
             "type": "text",
             "text": "The photons of the radiation have more energy than photons of visible light."
            }
           ]
          }
         ]
        }
       ],
       "rationale": {
        "type": "doc",
        "content": [
         {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
           {
            "marks": [],
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The photon energies of infrared and visible light are put in the wrong order (the frequency of infrared is lower)"
           }
          ]
         }
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "key": "D",
       "cells": [
        {
         "type": "doc",
         "content": [
          {
           "type": "paragraph",
           "content": [
            {
             "marks": [],
             "type": "text",
             "text": "The molecules reflect the radiation back towards the surface."
            }
           ]
          }
         ]
        }
       ],
       "rationale": {
        "type": "doc",
        "content": [
         {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
           {
            "marks": [],
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The \"greenhouse gases reflect heat\" misconception; the process is absorption and re-emission in all directions, not reflection"
           }
          ]
         }
        ]
       }
      }
     ],
     "correct_key": "A",
     "explanation": {
      "type": "doc",
      "content": [
       {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
         {
          "marks": [],
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Resonance model: the energy transfer is greatest when the frequency of the infrared radiation is close to the natural vibration frequency of the molecule; the molecule absorbs the radiation and then re-emits it in all directions."
         }
        ]
       }
      ]
     },
